var DG_0703_EVAL = `
<figure>
<svg class="diagram" viewBox="0 0 760 470" role="img" aria-labelledby="m0703at m0703ad">
  <title id="m0703at">How AWS KMS decides whether a request to use a key is allowed</title>
  <desc id="m0703ad">A request to call kms:Decrypt on a key in account 111122223333 is first checked for an explicit Deny in the key policy, identity policies, SCPs or RCPs, permissions boundaries, session policies and VPC endpoint policies; any match denies it. If nothing denies it, two cases follow. Same account: any one of three allow paths is enough: A, the key policy names the principal; B, the key policy contains the root statement that delegates to IAM and an IAM policy allows the action; C, a grant names the principal as grantee. Cross-account, for a caller in 444455556666: the key policy in 111122223333 must allow that account or its role, and the IAM policy in 444455556666 must also allow the role to use the key ARN. The caller must use the key ARN, only key-use operations work across accounts, and AWS managed keys can never be used this way.</desc>
  <defs><marker id="m0703a-ar" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path class="dg-arrow" d="M0 0 L10 5 L0 10 z"/></marker></defs>
  <rect class="dg-edge" x="130" y="12" width="500" height="40" rx="8"/>
  <text class="dg-tb" x="146" y="37">Request: kms:Decrypt on a key in 111122223333</text>
  <path class="dg-line" d="M380 52 V72" marker-end="url(#m0703a-ar)"/>

  <rect class="dg-bad" x="16" y="74" width="728" height="58" rx="8"/>
  <text class="dg-tb" x="28" y="97">Step 1: does any explicit Deny match?  Yes → DENIED</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="28" y="118">key policy · IAM identity policy · SCP / RCP · permissions boundary · session policy · VPC endpoint policy</text>
  <path class="dg-line" d="M194 132 V158" marker-end="url(#m0703a-ar)"/>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="204" y="150">no Deny</text>
  <path class="dg-line" d="M566 132 V158" marker-end="url(#m0703a-ar)"/>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="576" y="150">no Deny</text>

  <rect class="dg-region" x="16" y="160" width="356" height="296" rx="10"/>
  <text class="dg-ta" x="28" y="182">Same account: any ONE allow is enough</text>
  <rect class="dg-good" x="32" y="194" width="324" height="56" rx="8"/>
  <text class="dg-tb" x="44" y="217">A. Key policy names the principal</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="44" y="237">e.g. Principal role/AppRole, kms:Decrypt</text>
  <text class="dg-ta" x="182" y="268">OR</text>
  <rect class="dg-good" x="32" y="276" width="324" height="56" rx="8"/>
  <text class="dg-tb" x="44" y="299">B. Root statement + IAM policy</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="44" y="319">key policy delegates; role policy allows</text>
  <text class="dg-ta" x="182" y="350">OR</text>
  <rect class="dg-good" x="32" y="358" width="324" height="56" rx="8"/>
  <text class="dg-tb" x="44" y="381">C. A grant names the principal</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="44" y="401">grantee + operations; no IAM needed</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="32" y="440">None of A, B or C → implicit deny</text>

  <rect class="dg-region" x="388" y="160" width="356" height="296" rx="10"/>
  <text class="dg-ta" x="400" y="182">Cross-account: BOTH sides must allow</text>
  <rect class="dg-info" x="404" y="194" width="324" height="64" rx="8"/>
  <text class="dg-tb" x="416" y="218">1. Key policy in 111122223333</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="416" y="240">allows account 444455556666 or its role</text>
  <text class="dg-ta" x="550" y="278">AND</text>
  <rect class="dg-info" x="404" y="286" width="324" height="64" rx="8"/>
  <text class="dg-tb" x="416" y="310">2. IAM policy in 444455556666</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="416" y="332">allows the role kms:Decrypt on the key ARN</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="404" y="378">The caller must use the key ARN, not an alias</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="404" y="396">Only key-use and grant operations work;</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="404" y="414">no PutKeyPolicy, no ScheduleKeyDeletion</text>
  <text class="dg-ts" x="404" y="440">AWS managed keys: impossible (fixed policy)</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>Figure M07-3a. Every KMS request is authorised against the key's own policy first. In the key's account there are three independent ways to be allowed; across accounts the owner's key policy and the caller's IAM policy must both say yes. An explicit Deny anywhere ends the evaluation.</figcaption>
</figure>`;

