LESSONS.push({
  id: "M07.10", title: "Backup as data protection", level: 300, minutes: 60,
  objectives: [
    "Design an AWS Backup plan (rules, windows, lifecycle, copy actions) and a tag-based backup selection for a multi-service workload",
    "Choose between Vault Lock governance and compliance mode, and configure a vault access policy and KMS keys so copies survive a compromised account",
    "Build ransomware-resilient backups with cross-account copies, a central backup account and logically air-gapped vaults",
    "Calculate warm versus cold storage cost for a retention policy, including the 90-day cold storage minimum",
    "Prove recoverability with restore testing and Backup Audit Manager, and run a ransomware recovery from clean copies"
  ],
  sections: [
    { type: "why", html: `
<p>The previous nine lessons protected data from being <em>read</em> by the wrong people: encryption at rest and in transit, KMS keys and key policies, secrets, classification and Object Lock. This lesson protects data from being <em>lost</em>. Confidentiality and integrity are worthless if the only copy of a database has been encrypted by ransomware, dropped by a bad migration script or deleted by a departing administrator. Availability of data is the third leg of the CIA triad, and backups are how you deliver it.</p>
<p>Here is the scenario that keeps security teams awake. An attacker phishes an engineer and steals long-term access keys with <code>AdministratorAccess</code> in the production account. Before encrypting anything, the attacker does what every modern ransomware crew does: finds the backups and deletes them. RDS automated snapshots, EBS snapshots, the AWS Backup recovery points in the same account: all gone in a few API calls, because the stolen credentials are allowed to delete them. Only then does the ransom note arrive. If every copy lived inside the compromised account, the company now has two choices: pay, or rebuild from nothing.</p>
<p>M04.03 established that <strong>replication is not a backup</strong>: replication faithfully copies deletions and corruption. A backup is a <em>point-in-time</em> copy that you can go back to, and for it to protect you it must be <strong>out of reach</strong> of whatever destroyed the original. In AWS that means three properties: the copy is in another <strong>account</strong> (a different blast radius for credentials), optionally in another <strong>Region</strong> (a different blast radius for infrastructure), and it is <strong>immutable</strong> for its retention period (no one can delete it early, not even the root user).</p>
<p>On SAA-C03 this shows up in Task 1.3 ("backups and replication", "data retention") and Task 2.2 ("design highly available and/or fault-tolerant architectures", backup strategies). Expect stems like "centrally manage backups for EC2, RDS and EFS across 50 accounts", "backups must be immutable for seven years to meet regulation", "protect backups from a compromised administrator" and "minimise cost for backups kept for a year". The answers are AWS Backup, backup plans, Vault Lock in compliance mode, cross-account copies, lifecycle to cold storage. This lesson teaches each of those, the arithmetic behind the cost choices, and how to prove that a backup actually restores. M20.05 covers AWS Backup as a storage service in more depth, and M33 builds disaster recovery strategies on top of it.</p>` },

    { type: "concept", title: "Concept: the AWS Backup building blocks", html: DG_0710_ARCH + `
<p><strong>AWS Backup</strong> is a fully managed, policy-based service that centralises and automates backups across AWS services. Instead of configuring RDS backup windows, EBS snapshot schedules (Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager), DynamoDB on-demand backups and EFS backups separately in every account, you write one policy that says <em>what</em> to protect, <em>how often</em>, <em>how long</em> to keep it and <em>where</em> to copy it, and AWS Backup runs it. Before going further, the vocabulary:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><strong>Backup plan</strong></td><td>A named policy made of one or more <strong>rules</strong>. A plan is a Regional resource in one account.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Backup rule</strong></td><td>One schedule and its settings: a cron or rate <strong>schedule expression</strong> (optionally with a time zone), a <strong>start window</strong> (how long after the scheduled time the job may start; default 8 hours, minimum 1 hour), a <strong>completion window</strong> (how long the job may run before it is cancelled; default 7 days), a <strong>target vault</strong>, a <strong>lifecycle</strong> (when to move to cold storage, when to delete), optional <strong>copy actions</strong> and tags to put on recovery points. A rule can also enable <strong>continuous backup</strong> for point-in-time recovery on supported services.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Backup selection</strong> (resource assignment)</td><td>Says which resources the plan protects: by ARN (wildcards allowed), by tag, or by conditions on tags, plus an exclusion list (<code>NotResources</code>). It names the <strong>IAM role</strong> AWS Backup assumes to create backups, by default <code>AWSBackupDefaultServiceRole</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Backup job</strong></td><td>One run of one rule against one resource. Its result is a <strong>recovery point</strong>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Recovery point</strong></td><td>A backup of one resource at one point in time: an EBS snapshot, an AMI, an RDS snapshot, an EFS or DynamoDB backup, an S3 backup and so on. Continuous backups produce a recovery point you can restore to any second inside the window.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Backup vault</strong></td><td>The container that stores and organises recovery points. Every vault has an <strong>encryption key</strong> (KMS), an optional <strong>vault access policy</strong> (a resource-based policy) and an optional <strong>Vault Lock</strong>. Each account has a <code>Default</code> vault per Region.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Copy job</strong></td><td>Copies a recovery point to another vault: another Region, another account, or both, with its own lifecycle in the destination.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Warm / cold storage</strong></td><td>Warm is the default tier, used for fast restores. Cold storage is a cheaper tier for long retention, billed for a <strong>minimum of 90 days</strong>, available only for supported resource types.</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h3>Which services can AWS Backup protect?</h3>
<p>The list keeps growing, so learn the categories rather than memorising it: compute (EC2 instances as AMIs, EBS volumes), databases (RDS for every engine, Aurora, DynamoDB, DocumentDB, Neptune, Redshift, Timestream), file and object storage (EFS, all four FSx file systems, S3 buckets), hybrid (Storage Gateway volumes, VMware virtual machines on premises or in VMware Cloud on AWS), plus SAP HANA on EC2, CloudFormation stacks and Amazon EKS clusters. Some services are under <strong>"full AWS Backup management"</strong> (for example EFS, DynamoDB with advanced features turned on, S3): AWS Backup owns the recovery point format, encrypts it with the <em>vault's</em> key and controls its permissions and lifecycle independently of the source service. Others (EBS, EC2, RDS, Aurora and other snapshot-based services) produce the service's own snapshot, which keeps the <em>source</em> resource's encryption. That difference matters for cross-account copies, as the AWS section explains.</p>

<h3>Selecting resources by tag</h3>
<p>Assigning resources by ARN works for a demo, but it breaks the moment someone creates a new database. Production designs assign by <strong>tag</strong>: every resource tagged <code>BackupTier=gold</code> is protected by the gold plan, automatically, from the moment it is created. Tags are the contract between application teams ("this matters") and the platform team ("then it gets backed up"). Combine this with a tag policy and an SCP that requires the tag (M06.08) and an AWS Config rule that finds untagged or unprotected resources, and new resources can't silently escape backup.</p>
<p>A selection can use two styles. <code>ListOfTags</code> entries are <strong>OR</strong>ed: a resource matching any one is selected. The newer <code>Conditions</code> block supports <code>StringEquals</code>, <code>StringNotEquals</code>, <code>StringLike</code> and <code>StringNotLike</code> on <code>aws:ResourceTag/&lt;key&gt;</code>, and its conditions are <strong>AND</strong>ed, so you can say "tagged gold <em>and not</em> Environment=dev". <code>Resources</code> and <code>NotResources</code> narrow the scope by ARN pattern, for example only RDS: <code>arn:aws:rds:*:*:db:*</code>.</p>

<h3>Three copies, three threats</h3>
<p>Figure M07-10a shows one plan producing three copies. Each copy defends against a different failure, and a common exam trap is choosing a copy that defends against the wrong one.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Copy</th><th>Protects against</th><th>Does NOT protect against</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>(1) Local vault, same account and Region</td><td>Accidental deletion of a table, a bad deployment, corrupted files; fastest restore</td><td>Region outage; anyone with admin rights in the account</td></tr>
<tr><td>(2) Another Region, same account</td><td>Regional disaster, which is the DR use case (M33)</td><td>Compromised credentials: the same admin can delete it</td></tr>
<tr><td>(3) Another account (central backup account) with Vault Lock</td><td>Compromised workload account, malicious insider, ransomware, early deletion by anyone</td><td>Nothing in the list above, if the account's own access is tightly controlled</td></tr>
</tbody></table>` },

    { type: "concept", title: "Concept: immutability and ransomware resilience", html: DG_0710_RANSOM + `
<h3>Backup vault access policies</h3>
<p>A <strong>vault access policy</strong> is a resource-based policy on a vault, written like any IAM policy (M05.03). It is evaluated together with the caller's identity policy, so an explicit <code>Deny</code> in the vault policy beats an <code>Allow</code> in <code>AdministratorAccess</code>. Two statements appear in almost every production vault: one that <strong>denies</strong> <code>backup:DeleteRecoveryPoint</code> and <code>backup:UpdateRecoveryPointLifecycle</code> for everyone except a break-glass role, and (on a destination vault) one that <strong>allows</strong> <code>backup:CopyIntoBackupVault</code> only from principals in your organization (<code>aws:PrincipalOrgID</code>). A vault policy alone is not immutability, though: an administrator of the vault's account can edit or remove the policy and then delete. That is the gap Vault Lock closes.</p>

<h3>AWS Backup Vault Lock</h3>
<p><strong>Vault Lock</strong> enforces a write-once-read-many (WORM) setting on a vault. Once a vault is locked, no one can delete a recovery point in it, or shorten its lifecycle, before it reaches its retention period. You also set a <strong>minimum</strong> and a <strong>maximum retention</strong> in days: any backup or copy job whose lifecycle falls outside that range <strong>fails</strong>, so nobody can sneak in a recovery point that expires after one day or one that you'll pay for for a century. The minimum also applies to recovery points already in the vault when the lock takes effect.</p>
<p>Vault Lock has two modes, chosen by a single parameter, <code>ChangeableForDays</code>:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Governance mode</strong> (you omit <code>ChangeableForDays</code>): the lock is enforced, but IAM principals with the right permission (<code>backup:DeleteBackupVaultLockConfiguration</code> or <code>backup:PutBackupVaultLockConfiguration</code>) can remove or change it. Use it to test a lock, or to stop accidental deletions while trusting your own administrators.</li>
  <li><strong>Compliance mode</strong> (you set <code>ChangeableForDays</code>, minimum 3 days, so a grace time of at least 72 hours): during this <strong>cooling-off period</strong> you can still change or delete the lock. When it ends, the lock becomes <strong>immutable</strong>: no user, not the root user and not AWS, can change or delete the lock, delete the locked recovery points before they expire, or delete the vault while it holds them. Use it for regulatory WORM retention and for ransomware protection.</li>
</ul>
<p>AWS Backup Vault Lock in compliance mode has been assessed by Cohasset Associates for SEC 17a-4(f), FINRA and CFTC record-keeping requirements, the same family of rules you met with S3 Object Lock (M07.09).</p>
<div class="callout warn">Compliance mode is a one-way door. If you lock a vault with a maximum retention of 3,650 days and then copy 500 TB into it with a ten-year lifecycle, you will pay for ten years of storage and nobody can stop it. Always test in governance mode, check the min/max values, and only then lock in compliance mode.</div>

<h3>What Vault Lock does not protect</h3>
<p>Locking the recovery points is necessary but not sufficient. Three things can still destroy your ability to restore:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The KMS key.</strong> A recovery point encrypted with a customer managed key that the attacker can schedule for deletion becomes unreadable 7 to 30 days later (M07.04). Keep the destination vault's key in the backup account, restrict <code>kms:ScheduleKeyDeletion</code> with an SCP, and alarm on key deletion events.</li>
  <li><strong>The account itself.</strong> Whoever controls the backup account controls its access. Give it its own small group of administrators, no workload, no long-term access keys, and an SCP that denies <code>backup:DeleteBackupVault</code>, <code>backup:DeleteRecoveryPoint</code>, <code>backup:PutBackupVaultAccessPolicy</code> and lock changes except for break-glass.</li>
  <li><strong>Dwell time.</strong> Attackers often sit in an environment for days or weeks before encrypting. If your locked copies expire after 7 days and the attacker had been inside for 10, every clean copy is gone. Retention must exceed realistic detection time.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Logically air-gapped vaults</h3>
<p>A <strong>logically air-gapped vault</strong> is a special vault type for the worst case: your own backup account is compromised, or you need to recover into a brand-new account. It is <strong>always locked in compliance mode</strong> (you give it a min and max retention when you create it), it is encrypted by default with an <strong>AWS owned key</strong> so there is no customer key for an attacker to delete (newer releases also allow a customer managed key), and you can <strong>share it with other accounts through AWS RAM</strong>. A recovery account that receives the share can restore directly from it without first copying the data, which shortens recovery time. AWS Backup can also integrate the air-gapped vault with <strong>multi-party approval</strong>, so that a group of approved people can grant a recovery account access even if the owning account is unusable. Supported resource types are narrower than for standard vaults and growing; check the feature table before you design around it.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Standard vault</th><th>Standard vault + Vault Lock (compliance)</th><th>Logically air-gapped vault</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Immutable?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, after the grace time</td><td>Yes, always</td></tr>
<tr><td>Encryption</td><td>Vault key or source key (depends on the service)</td><td>Same</td><td>AWS owned key by default</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recovery from another account</td><td>Copy out first</td><td>Copy out first</td><td>Restore directly through a RAM share</td></tr>
<tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Operational restores</td><td>Central backup account, compliance retention</td><td>Last line of defence, isolated recovery</td></tr>
</tbody></table>` },
    { type: "workflow", title: "Workflow: from schedule to restore", html: `
<h3>What happens on every scheduled run</h3>
<ol class="flow">
  <li><strong>Schedule fires.</strong> At 02:00 Europe/Dublin the rule's cron expression triggers. AWS Backup evaluates the selection <em>now</em>, so a resource tagged <code>BackupTier=gold</code> an hour ago is included and one whose tag was removed is not.</li>
  <li><strong>Job starts inside the start window.</strong> AWS Backup assumes the selection's IAM role and starts one backup job per resource within the start window (here 60 minutes). If a job can't start in the window (for example another backup of the same resource is still running), it is marked <code>EXPIRED</code> and not retried until the next schedule.</li>
  <li><strong>The service takes the backup.</strong> EBS takes an incremental snapshot, RDS a DB snapshot, DynamoDB and EFS a backup managed by AWS Backup. Most are <strong>incremental</strong> after the first full backup, so you pay for changed blocks. The job must finish inside the completion window or it is cancelled.</li>
  <li><strong>Recovery point lands in the vault</strong>, encrypted (with the vault key or the source key, depending on the service), tagged with <code>RecoveryPointTags</code>, with a lifecycle that counts from its creation date.</li>
  <li><strong>Copy actions run.</strong> When the backup completes, each copy action starts a copy job to its destination vault (another Region, another account). The copy is re-encrypted with the destination's key and gets the destination lifecycle.</li>
  <li><strong>Lifecycle moves and deletes.</strong> After <code>MoveToColdStorageAfterDays</code> supported recovery points move to cold storage; after <code>DeleteAfterDays</code> they are deleted, unless a Vault Lock minimum or a <strong>legal hold</strong> keeps them.</li>
  <li><strong>Events and reports.</strong> Every job state change goes to EventBridge and optionally an SNS topic; CloudTrail records every API call; Backup Audit Manager evaluates compliance and writes daily reports.</li>
</ol>

<h3>Restore flow</h3>
<ol class="flow">
  <li><strong>Pick the recovery point.</strong> For an operational mistake, the newest point before the mistake. For ransomware, the newest point <em>before the attacker arrived</em>, which you find from CloudTrail and GuardDuty, not from the date the files were encrypted.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose where to restore.</strong> A restore creates a <strong>new</strong> resource (a new volume, DB instance or table) or, for EFS and S3, writes into a new directory or as new object versions; it doesn't roll the original back in place. After ransomware, restore into a clean account, not the compromised one.</li>
  <li><strong>Supply restore metadata.</strong> <code>get-recovery-point-restore-metadata</code> returns the source settings; you override what must change (subnet group, security groups, instance class, KMS key).</li>
  <li><strong>Start the restore job</strong> with a role that has the restore permissions, wait for <code>COMPLETED</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Validate and cut over.</strong> Check row counts or checksums, run application smoke tests, then repoint DNS or connection strings. Recovery time (RTO) includes all of this, not only the restore job.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout tip">A backup you have never restored is a hope, not a backup. Restore testing (next section) runs this restore flow on a schedule and records how long it takes.</div>` },

    { type: "aws", title: "How it works on AWS: encryption, copies, organizations and proof", html: `
<h3>Encryption of recovery points</h3>
<p>Every vault is created with a KMS key: the AWS managed key <code>aws/backup</code> by default, or a customer managed key you choose (only at creation; you can't change a vault's key later). For resources under full AWS Backup management (EFS, DynamoDB with advanced features, S3, Timestream, VMware and others), the recovery point is encrypted with the <strong>vault's key</strong>, so even data that was unencrypted at the source ends up encrypted. For snapshot-based services (EBS, EC2 AMIs, RDS, Aurora, DocumentDB, Neptune), the snapshot keeps the <strong>source resource's encryption</strong>: an unencrypted RDS instance produces an unencrypted snapshot. Fix that at the source (M07.06: snapshot, copy with encryption, restore).</p>

<h3>Cross-Region copies</h3>
<p>A copy action names a destination vault ARN in another Region. The copy is encrypted with the destination vault's key (KMS keys are Regional; a multi-Region key from M07.03 is not required). Cross-Region data transfer is charged per GB, and the destination stores a full first copy. Use it for Regional DR; it is the backup-and-restore strategy that M33 compares with pilot light, warm standby and multi-site.</p>

<h3>Cross-account copies</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Both accounts must be in the <strong>same AWS Organization</strong>, and cross-account backup must be turned on once, in the management account: <code>aws backup update-global-settings --global-settings isCrossAccountBackupEnabled=true</code>.</li>
  <li>The <strong>destination vault's access policy</strong> must allow <code>backup:CopyIntoBackupVault</code> from the source accounts (usually with <code>aws:PrincipalOrgID</code>).</li>
  <li>For snapshot-based services, the source must be encrypted with a <strong>customer managed key</strong> whose key policy lets the destination account use it. Snapshots encrypted with AWS managed keys (<code>aws/ebs</code>, <code>aws/rds</code>) can't be copied to another account (M07.06), because AWS managed key policies can't be edited (M07.03).</li>
  <li>For some services (for example RDS, Aurora, DocumentDB, Neptune) a single copy can't change Region <em>and</em> account at the same time; copy to the other account in the same Region, then copy onward.</li>
  <li>A copy job is started by the <em>source</em> account. The destination's Vault Lock then protects it from the source account and from everybody else.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Organization-wide backup</h3>
<p>Writing the same plan in 80 accounts doesn't scale. M06.08 (Example 5) showed an <strong>Organizations backup policy</strong> that deploys a plan with tag-based selection and copy actions to every account in an OU; members can't edit it. Pair it with a <strong>delegated administrator</strong> for AWS Backup (the central backup account) so job monitoring, Audit Manager and restore testing aren't run from the management account. We won't repeat the policy syntax here.</p>

<h3>Continuous backup and point-in-time recovery</h3>
<p>For supported services (for example RDS, Aurora, S3 and SAP HANA on EC2), a rule with <code>EnableContinuousBackup: true</code> keeps a continuous log so you can restore to any second within the retention, up to <strong>35 days</strong>. Pair a continuous rule (short, precise RPO) with snapshot rules (long retention). DynamoDB has its own point-in-time recovery (up to 35 days) configured on the table; AWS Backup protects DynamoDB with scheduled backups.</p>

<h3>Lifecycle and cold storage rules</h3>
<ul>
  <li>Cold storage is available only for supported resource types (for example EFS, DynamoDB with advanced features, Timestream, SAP HANA, VMware, and EBS through the snapshot archive tier with <code>OptInToArchiveForSupportedResources</code>). For other types, such as RDS, Aurora and S3, the cold transition is ignored.</li>
  <li>A cold recovery point is billed for at least <strong>90 days</strong>; deleting it earlier incurs a pro-rated charge for the remaining days. The API enforces <code>DeleteAfterDays ≥ MoveToColdStorageAfterDays + 90</code>.</li>
  <li>Cold storage restores cost more per GB and take longer; an archived EBS snapshot is stored as a <strong>full</strong> snapshot, not an increment, and must be restored to the standard tier before use (hours).</li>
</ul>

<h3>Legal holds</h3>
<p>An AWS Backup <strong>legal hold</strong> stops selected recovery points (by vault, resource type or date range) from being deleted by their lifecycle until the hold is released, which is how you preserve evidence for litigation or an investigation without rewriting plans.</p>

<h3>Restore testing</h3>
<p><strong>Restore testing</strong> automates the restore flow. A <strong>restore testing plan</strong> has a schedule and a recovery point selection (<code>LATEST_WITHIN_WINDOW</code> or <code>RANDOM_WITHIN_WINDOW</code> from the last N days, from chosen vaults). <strong>Restore testing selections</strong> name the resource type, the protected resources (by ARN or tag) and restore metadata overrides such as a dedicated subnet group. AWS Backup restores the resource, publishes a <em>Restore Job State Change</em> event so you can run your own validation (for example a Lambda function that queries the restored database and calls <code>PutRestoreValidationResult</code>), then <strong>deletes the restored resource</strong> after the validation window. Each test records the restore duration, which is your measured restore time against RTO.</p>

<h3>Backup Audit Manager</h3>
<p><strong>AWS Backup Audit Manager</strong> evaluates your backups against <strong>frameworks</strong> of <strong>controls</strong> (it uses AWS Config, which must be recording the relevant resource types). Example controls: resources are protected by a backup plan; backup frequency and retention meet a minimum; recovery points are encrypted; resources have a cross-Region or cross-account copy; vaults are locked; recovery points can't be deleted manually; the last recovery point is recent enough; restore time meets a target. <strong>Report plans</strong> deliver daily CSV or JSON reports (jobs, copies, restores, control compliance) to S3, for one account or, from the delegated administrator, across the organization. That is the evidence an auditor asks for.</p>

<h3>Exam-relevant quotas and defaults</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Start window</td><td>Default 8 hours, minimum 1 hour</td></tr>
<tr><td>Completion window</td><td>Default 7 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cold storage minimum</td><td>90 days (DeleteAfterDays ≥ cold transition + 90)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continuous backup retention</td><td>Up to 35 days</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vault Lock compliance grace time</td><td>At least 3 days (72 hours), via ChangeableForDays</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cross-account copy</td><td>Same organization; enabled in the management account</td></tr>
<tr><td>Default vault key</td><td><code>aws/backup</code> (AWS managed); customer managed key chosen at vault creation</td></tr>
</tbody></table>` },
    { type: "examples", html: `
<h3>Example 1: a backup plan with copies, cold storage and continuous backup</h3>
<p>Workload account 111122223333, Region eu-west-1. Save as <code>plan.json</code>:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "BackupPlanName": "gold-plan",
  "Rules": [
    {
      "RuleName": "daily-35d-with-copies",
      "TargetBackupVaultName": "prod-vault",
      "ScheduleExpression": "cron(0 2 ? * * *)",
      "ScheduleExpressionTimezone": "Europe/Dublin",
      "StartWindowMinutes": 60,
      "CompletionWindowMinutes": 480,
      "Lifecycle": { "DeleteAfterDays": 35 },
      "RecoveryPointTags": { "BackupTier": "gold", "Rule": "daily" },
      "CopyActions": [
        {
          "DestinationBackupVaultArn": "arn:aws:backup:eu-central-1:111122223333:backup-vault:prod-vault-dr",
          "Lifecycle": { "DeleteAfterDays": 35 }
        },
        {
          "DestinationBackupVaultArn": "arn:aws:backup:eu-west-1:444455556666:backup-vault:central-locked-vault",
          "Lifecycle": { "DeleteAfterDays": 90 }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "RuleName": "monthly-1y-cold",
      "TargetBackupVaultName": "prod-vault",
      "ScheduleExpression": "cron(0 4 1 * ? *)",
      "ScheduleExpressionTimezone": "Europe/Dublin",
      "StartWindowMinutes": 60,
      "CompletionWindowMinutes": 720,
      "Lifecycle": {
        "MoveToColdStorageAfterDays": 30,
        "DeleteAfterDays": 365,
        "OptInToArchiveForSupportedResources": true
      }
    },
    {
      "RuleName": "continuous-pitr-14d",
      "TargetBackupVaultName": "prod-vault",
      "ScheduleExpression": "cron(0 5 ? * * *)",
      "EnableContinuousBackup": true,
      "Lifecycle": { "DeleteAfterDays": 14 }
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<p>Read it rule by rule. <code>cron(0 2 ? * * *)</code> is 02:00 every day (fields: minute, hour, day-of-month, month, day-of-week, year; one of the two day fields must be <code>?</code>). The daily rule keeps points 35 days locally and copies each one twice: to eu-central-1 in the same account (Region DR) and to the locked vault in the backup account, which keeps it 90 days. The monthly rule runs at 04:00 on day 1 and moves supported resources to cold storage after 30 days, deleting after 365 (the API accepts it because 365 ≥ 30 + 90). The continuous rule gives point-in-time recovery for 14 days on RDS, Aurora and S3.</p>
<pre><code>aws backup create-backup-plan --backup-plan file://plan.json --region eu-west-1
{
    "BackupPlanId": "8a1b2c3d-4e5f-6a7b-8c9d-0e1f2a3b4c5d",
    "BackupPlanArn": "arn:aws:backup:eu-west-1:111122223333:backup-plan:8a1b2c3d-4e5f-6a7b-8c9d-0e1f2a3b4c5d",
    "CreationDate": "2026-10-07T08:41:17.402000+00:00",
    "VersionId": "ZmYzNjE0ZDItN2QxYS00Y2E2LWI1MjYtOGQ1NDQ3ZWRkYTZm"
}</code></pre>

<h3>Example 2: tag-based backup selection</h3>
<p>Save as <code>selection.json</code>. All resources (<code>"*"</code>) whose tags match <em>both</em> conditions:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "SelectionName": "gold-not-dev",
  "IamRoleArn": "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/service-role/AWSBackupDefaultServiceRole",
  "Resources": ["*"],
  "Conditions": {
    "StringEquals": [
      { "ConditionKey": "aws:ResourceTag/BackupTier", "ConditionValue": "gold" }
    ],
    "StringNotEquals": [
      { "ConditionKey": "aws:ResourceTag/Environment", "ConditionValue": "dev" }
    ]
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre><code>aws backup create-backup-selection \\
    --backup-plan-id 8a1b2c3d-4e5f-6a7b-8c9d-0e1f2a3b4c5d \\
    --backup-selection file://selection.json
{
    "SelectionId": "5f6e7d8c-9b0a-4c1d-8e2f-3a4b5c6d7e8f",
    "BackupPlanId": "8a1b2c3d-4e5f-6a7b-8c9d-0e1f2a3b4c5d",
    "CreationDate": "2026-10-07T08:43:02.775000+00:00"
}</code></pre>
<p>Opt-in matters too: <code>aws backup describe-region-settings</code> shows which resource types AWS Backup may protect in this Region (<code>ResourceTypeOptInPreference</code>). A type that is opted out is silently skipped by every plan.</p>

<h3>Example 3: the central locked vault (backup account 444455556666)</h3>
<pre><code># Once, in the management account:
aws backup update-global-settings --global-settings isCrossAccountBackupEnabled=true

# In the backup account: vault with its own customer managed key
aws backup create-backup-vault --backup-vault-name central-locked-vault \\
    --encryption-key-arn arn:aws:kms:eu-west-1:444455556666:key/1234abcd-12ab-34cd-56ef-1234567890ab
{
    "BackupVaultName": "central-locked-vault",
    "BackupVaultArn": "arn:aws:backup:eu-west-1:444455556666:backup-vault:central-locked-vault",
    "CreationDate": "2026-10-07T09:12:44.118000+00:00"
}

aws backup put-backup-vault-access-policy --backup-vault-name central-locked-vault \\
    --policy file://vault-policy.json</code></pre>
<p><code>vault-policy.json</code> allows copies in from the organization and denies deletes for everyone except a break-glass role:</p>
<pre><code>{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "AllowCopyIntoFromOrg",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "*" },
      "Action": "backup:CopyIntoBackupVault",
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": { "StringEquals": { "aws:PrincipalOrgID": "o-a1b2c3d4e5" } }
    },
    {
      "Sid": "DenyDeleteExceptBreakGlass",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Principal": { "AWS": "*" },
      "Action": [
        "backup:DeleteRecoveryPoint",
        "backup:UpdateRecoveryPointLifecycle"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnNotLike": { "aws:PrincipalArn": "arn:aws:iam::444455556666:role/BackupBreakGlass" }
      }
    }
  ]
}</code></pre>
<p>Now the lock. Test in governance mode first (no <code>--changeable-for-days</code>), then switch to compliance mode:</p>
<pre><code># Governance mode: removable by principals allowed backup:DeleteBackupVaultLockConfiguration
aws backup put-backup-vault-lock-configuration --backup-vault-name central-locked-vault \\
    --min-retention-days 7 --max-retention-days 400

# Compliance mode: changeable for 3 days, then immutable
aws backup put-backup-vault-lock-configuration --backup-vault-name central-locked-vault \\
    --min-retention-days 7 --max-retention-days 400 --changeable-for-days 3

aws backup describe-backup-vault --backup-vault-name central-locked-vault
{
    "BackupVaultName": "central-locked-vault",
    "BackupVaultArn": "arn:aws:backup:eu-west-1:444455556666:backup-vault:central-locked-vault",
    "VaultType": "BACKUP_VAULT",
    "EncryptionKeyArn": "arn:aws:kms:eu-west-1:444455556666:key/1234abcd-12ab-34cd-56ef-1234567890ab",
    "CreationDate": "2026-10-07T09:12:44.118000+00:00",
    "NumberOfRecoveryPoints": 0,
    "Locked": true,
    "MinRetentionDays": 7,
    "MaxRetentionDays": 400,
    "LockDate": "2026-10-10T09:15:02.530000+00:00"
}</code></pre>
<p><code>LockDate</code> is when the grace time ends and the configuration becomes immutable. Until then, <code>aws backup delete-backup-vault-lock-configuration</code> still works; afterwards it returns an error for everyone, including the root user. Every copy from Example 1 (90 days) fits inside 7–400 days. A copy action with <code>DeleteAfterDays: 5</code> would fail, and so would one with 3,650.</p>

<h3>Example 4: what does a year of retention cost? (warm vs cold)</h3>
<p><strong>Illustrative prices only</strong> (round numbers for the arithmetic; real prices vary by Region, resource type and over time, so check the AWS Backup pricing page): warm backup storage <strong>$0.05 per GB-month</strong>, cold storage <strong>$0.01 per GB-month</strong>, restore from cold <strong>$0.03 per GB</strong>. One month = 30 days.</p>
<p><strong>Scenario.</strong> An EFS file system gets one monthly recovery point, kept for 12 months. Assume each recovery point is billed at 400 GB in either tier.</p>
` + DG_0710_LIFE + `
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Points in warm (steady state)</th><th>Points in cold</th><th>Monthly cost</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A: warm for 365 days</td><td>12 × 400 GB = 4,800 GB</td><td>0</td><td>4,800 × $0.05 = <strong>$240.00</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>B: cold after 30 days, delete at 365</td><td>1 × 400 GB = 400 GB → $20.00</td><td>11 × 400 GB = 4,400 GB → $44.00</td><td>$20.00 + $44.00 = <strong>$64.00</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Saving: $240 − $64 = <strong>$176 per month</strong> (176 ÷ 240 = 73%), or $2,112 a year. At steady state the 12 points are 0, 30, 60 … 330 days old; only the newest is younger than 30 days, so 1 is warm and 11 are cold.</p>
<p><strong>The 90-day minimum.</strong> A rule with <code>MoveToColdStorageAfterDays: 30</code> and <code>DeleteAfterDays: 60</code> is rejected: the earliest legal deletion is 30 + 90 = <strong>120</strong> days. If someone with permission deletes a cold 400 GB point after only 40 days in cold, AWS bills the remaining 90 − 40 = 50 days: 400 × $0.01 × 50 ÷ 30 = <strong>$6.67</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>When is cold not worth it?</strong> Once in cold you pay at least 90 days × $0.01 ÷ 30 = $0.03 per GB. Staying warm costs $0.05 ÷ 30 per GB per day, so $0.03 buys 0.03 ÷ (0.05 ÷ 30) = <strong>18 days</strong> of warm storage. With these prices a point that has fewer than 18 days of life left is cheaper left warm; and because the API forces 90 days in cold anyway, short retentions (for example 35-day dailies) simply stay warm.</p>
<p><strong>Restore cost.</strong> Restoring one 400 GB point from cold: 400 × $0.03 = $12, plus a longer restore time. Cheap storage is the right choice for audit retention, not for the copies you'd restore during an outage.</p>
<div class="callout warn">The "billed at 400 GB in either tier" assumption hides a real trap. Warm backups of EBS and many other services are incremental, but an EBS snapshot moved to the archive tier is stored as a <strong>full</strong> snapshot. Ten 50 GB increments of a 1 TB volume cost about 500 GB warm but about 10 TB if every one is archived. Check how each resource type is billed in cold before you assume savings.</div>

<h3>Example 5: a weekly restore test</h3>
<pre><code>aws backup create-restore-testing-plan --restore-testing-plan '{
  "RestoreTestingPlanName": "weekly_gold_restore_test",
  "ScheduleExpression": "cron(0 6 ? * SAT *)",
  "ScheduleExpressionTimezone": "Europe/Dublin",
  "StartWindowHours": 4,
  "RecoveryPointSelection": {
    "Algorithm": "LATEST_WITHIN_WINDOW",
    "IncludeVaults": ["*"],
    "RecoveryPointTypes": ["SNAPSHOT"],
    "SelectionWindowDays": 7
  }
}'

aws backup create-restore-testing-selection \\
    --restore-testing-plan-name weekly_gold_restore_test \\
    --restore-testing-selection '{
  "RestoreTestingSelectionName": "gold_rds",
  "ProtectedResourceType": "RDS",
  "ProtectedResourceConditions": {
    "StringEquals": [ { "Key": "aws:ResourceTag/BackupTier", "Value": "gold" } ]
  },
  "IamRoleArn": "arn:aws:iam::111122223333:role/service-role/AWSBackupDefaultServiceRole",
  "ValidationWindowHours": 4,
  "RestoreMetadataOverrides": { "dbsubnetgroupname": "restore-test-subnets" }
}'</code></pre>
<p>Every Saturday the latest RDS recovery point from the past week is restored into an isolated subnet group. An EventBridge rule on <em>Restore Job State Change</em> = <code>COMPLETED</code> triggers a Lambda function that connects, counts rows in the <code>orders</code> table and calls <code>aws backup put-restore-validation-result --restore-job-id … --validation-status SUCCESSFUL</code>. After 4 hours the restored instance is deleted automatically. The restore job's duration is your measured restore time.</p>` },

    { type: "usecases", html: `
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>What you'd choose</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>One team must back up EC2, RDS, DynamoDB and EFS on one schedule with one report</td><td>AWS Backup plan with a tag-based selection</td><td>One policy across services instead of four service-specific tools</td></tr>
<tr><td>80 accounts must all follow the same backup standard; members can't weaken it</td><td>Organizations backup policy (M06.08) + AWS Backup delegated administrator</td><td>Central definition, inherited per OU, not editable in member accounts</td></tr>
<tr><td>Broker-dealer must keep trade records 7 years, unalterable</td><td>Vault Lock in compliance mode (min retention 2,555 days) in a dedicated account; cold storage where supported</td><td>Assessed for SEC 17a-4(f); nobody can shorten retention</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ransomware protection: assume prod admin credentials will be stolen</td><td>Cross-account copy to a central backup account with Vault Lock and its own KMS key; logically air-gapped vault shared via RAM for recovery</td><td>The attacker's credentials don't work there, and the lock blocks even insiders</td></tr>
<tr><td>Restore an RDS database to the state 37 minutes ago</td><td>Continuous backup (point-in-time recovery, up to 35 days)</td><td>Snapshots give a daily RPO; continuous gives seconds</td></tr>
<tr><td>Auditor asks for proof that backups are taken, encrypted, copied and restorable</td><td>Backup Audit Manager framework + report plan; restore testing records</td><td>Continuous, automatic evidence instead of screenshots</td></tr>
</tbody></table>` },

    { type: "demo", title: "Guided practice: back up and restore a DynamoDB table, then test a lock", html: `
<p>Run in AWS CloudShell in a sandbox account. Cost: cents (a tiny on-demand table and its backup). Everything is deleted at the end. Do <strong>not</strong> use <code>--changeable-for-days</code> in this demo: a compliance-mode lock can't be undone after its grace time.</p>
<pre><code>export AWS_REGION=eu-west-1
ACCT=$(aws sts get-caller-identity --query Account --output text)

# 1. A small table with two items
aws dynamodb create-table --table-name academy-orders \\
    --attribute-definitions AttributeName=id,AttributeType=S \\
    --key-schema AttributeName=id,KeyType=HASH --billing-mode PAY_PER_REQUEST
aws dynamodb wait table-exists --table-name academy-orders
aws dynamodb put-item --table-name academy-orders --item '{"id":{"S":"o-1"},"total":{"N":"42"}}'
aws dynamodb put-item --table-name academy-orders --item '{"id":{"S":"o-2"},"total":{"N":"17"}}'

# 2. A role AWS Backup can assume
aws iam create-role --role-name academy-backup-role --assume-role-policy-document \\
  '{"Version":"2012-10-17","Statement":[{"Effect":"Allow","Principal":{"Service":"backup.amazonaws.com"},"Action":"sts:AssumeRole"}]}'
aws iam attach-role-policy --role-name academy-backup-role \\
  --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSBackupServiceRolePolicyForBackup
aws iam attach-role-policy --role-name academy-backup-role \\
  --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSBackupServiceRolePolicyForRestores
ROLE=arn:aws:iam::$ACCT:role/academy-backup-role

# 3. A vault (default aws/backup key) and an on-demand backup kept 7 days
aws backup create-backup-vault --backup-vault-name academy-vault
JOB=$(aws backup start-backup-job --backup-vault-name academy-vault \\
    --resource-arn arn:aws:dynamodb:$AWS_REGION:$ACCT:table/academy-orders \\
    --iam-role-arn $ROLE --lifecycle DeleteAfterDays=7 --query BackupJobId --output text)
aws backup describe-backup-job --backup-job-id $JOB --query "[State,RecoveryPointArn]"
# repeat until State is COMPLETED (usually a minute or two)
RP=$(aws backup describe-backup-job --backup-job-id $JOB --query RecoveryPointArn --output text)

# 4. Simulate the disaster, then restore into a NEW table
aws dynamodb delete-item --table-name academy-orders --key '{"id":{"S":"o-1"}}'
aws backup get-recovery-point-restore-metadata --backup-vault-name academy-vault \\
    --recovery-point-arn $RP --query RestoreMetadata
# use the table-name key it returns; for DynamoDB it is targetTableName
RJ=$(aws backup start-restore-job --recovery-point-arn $RP --iam-role-arn $ROLE \\
    --metadata targetTableName=academy-orders-restored --query RestoreJobId --output text)
aws backup describe-restore-job --restore-job-id $RJ --query Status
aws dynamodb scan --table-name academy-orders-restored --select COUNT   # expect Count 2

# 5. Governance-mode lock on an empty vault, inspect it, remove it
aws backup create-backup-vault --backup-vault-name academy-lock-test
aws backup put-backup-vault-lock-configuration --backup-vault-name academy-lock-test \\
    --min-retention-days 1 --max-retention-days 30
aws backup describe-backup-vault --backup-vault-name academy-lock-test \\
    --query "[Locked,MinRetentionDays,MaxRetentionDays,LockDate]"
aws backup delete-backup-vault-lock-configuration --backup-vault-name academy-lock-test

# 6. Clean up
aws backup delete-recovery-point --backup-vault-name academy-vault --recovery-point-arn $RP
aws backup delete-backup-vault --backup-vault-name academy-vault
aws backup delete-backup-vault --backup-vault-name academy-lock-test
aws dynamodb delete-table --table-name academy-orders
aws dynamodb delete-table --table-name academy-orders-restored
aws iam detach-role-policy --role-name academy-backup-role \\
  --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSBackupServiceRolePolicyForBackup
aws iam detach-role-policy --role-name academy-backup-role \\
  --policy-arn arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/service-role/AWSBackupServiceRolePolicyForRestores
aws iam delete-role --role-name academy-backup-role</code></pre>
<p>What to notice: in step 4 the restore created a <em>new</em> table with both items, while the original still lacks <code>o-1</code>; in step 5 the describe output shows <code>LockDate</code> as null, because a governance-mode lock never becomes immutable; and in CloudTrail (Event history, source <code>backup.amazonaws.com</code>) every call you made is recorded. If DynamoDB advanced features for AWS Backup are off in your account, the recovery point is a native DynamoDB backup; it works the same way for this exercise.</p>` },
    { type: "casestudy", title: "Case study: Corvane Logistics recovers from ransomware", html: `
<p><strong>Situation.</strong> Corvane Logistics (fictional) runs its route-planning platform in one production account: EC2 application servers with EBS volumes, an EFS share of shipment documents, and an RDS for PostgreSQL database encrypted with a customer managed key. Eight months earlier, after an architecture review, the platform team had added a central backup account (444455556666) with a compliance-mode locked vault (min 7, max 400 days), daily cross-account copies kept 60 days, a monthly restore test and an SCP denying KMS key deletion outside a break-glass role. The local vault in the production account had no lock, "for flexibility".</p>
<p><strong>Incident.</strong> On a Monday at 03:10, monitoring showed the application failing. Files on EFS had been renamed with a new extension, the database was gone and a ransom note sat in an S3 bucket. CloudTrail told the story:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>When</th><th>Attacker action (stolen CI access keys with admin rights)</th><th>Outcome</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Day −9</td><td>First API call from an unfamiliar IP; created an IAM user for persistence</td><td>GuardDuty raised a medium finding that nobody triaged</td></tr>
<tr><td>Day −1, 23:40</td><td><code>DeleteRecoveryPoint</code> on all 412 points in the local vault; <code>DeleteDBSnapshot</code> on manual snapshots</td><td>Succeeded: no lock, no deny</td></tr>
<tr><td>Day −1, 23:52</td><td><code>DeleteRecoveryPoint</code> and <code>PutBackupVaultAccessPolicy</code> against the central vault</td><td><code>AccessDenied</code>: the prod keys are not principals in 444455556666</td></tr>
<tr><td>Day −1, 23:58</td><td><code>ScheduleKeyDeletion</code> on the RDS key</td><td>Denied by the SCP</td></tr>
<tr><td>Day 0, 01:15</td><td>Ransomware binary on EC2 encrypted EFS files; <code>DeleteDBInstance</code> with skip final snapshot</td><td>Production down</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Recovery.</strong> The incident team treated the production account as hostile and did not restore into it. They created a fresh recovery account from Account Factory and, from the backup account, copied recovery points from Day −10 (before the first malicious call) into the recovery account's vault. They chose Day −10, not Day −1, because the persistence user and possibly tampered AMIs existed from Day −9 on. RDS and EFS were restored from those copies; EC2 was rebuilt from the golden AMI pipeline rather than from the instance backups, then shipment documents created after Day −10 were re-imported from partners' EDI feeds. The monthly restore tests meant the runbook, subnet groups and parameter overrides already existed.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Target</th><th>Achieved</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>RTO (service back)</td><td>24 h</td><td>14 h</td></tr>
<tr><td>Data restored from backup</td><td>-</td><td>Day −10 point; 9 days of documents re-imported from partners, 2 days of route edits re-entered by hand</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ransom paid</td><td>-</td><td>None</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Lessons learned.</strong> (1) The account boundary did the real work: the attacker's admin keys were useless against the central vault. (2) Dwell time nearly beat them: the first design review had proposed 7-day copy retention, which would have left no clean copy at all, so retention is now 90 days and the GuardDuty finding pipeline pages someone. (3) The unlocked local vault was the first thing deleted; it now has a governance-mode lock and the SCP denies <code>DeleteRecoveryPoint</code> in workload accounts. (4) Restoring into a clean account was slower than restoring in place, but it avoided re-infection. (5) A logically air-gapped vault shared with a pre-built recovery account was added, so the next recovery doesn't need a copy step. Long term, the team is moving the platform to a warm-standby DR design (M33).</p>` },

    { type: "exam", html: `
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stem says…</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>"Centrally manage and automate backups across EC2, EBS, RDS, DynamoDB, EFS"</td><td><strong>AWS Backup</strong> backup plan</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Automatically back up new resources without manual steps"</td><td><strong>Tag-based</strong> backup selection</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Backups must not be deleted by anyone, including the root user, for N years"</td><td>Vault Lock in <strong>compliance mode</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>"Prevent accidental deletion, but admins may remove the protection later"</td><td>Vault Lock in <strong>governance mode</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>"Protect backups if the production account is compromised"</td><td><strong>Cross-account copy</strong> to a separate backup account (+ Vault Lock); logically air-gapped vault</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Recover from a Regional outage"</td><td><strong>Cross-Region copy</strong> (backup and restore DR, M33)</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Enforce a backup standard across all accounts in the organization"</td><td>Organizations <strong>backup policy</strong> (M06.08)</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Reduce cost of backups retained for years"</td><td>Lifecycle to <strong>cold storage</strong> (supported types; 90-day minimum)</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Restore to any point in the last few days"</td><td><strong>Continuous backup</strong> / point-in-time recovery (up to 35 days)</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Prove backups are compliant / restorable"</td><td><strong>Backup Audit Manager</strong>; <strong>restore testing</strong></td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h3>Governance vs compliance mode</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Governance mode</th><th>Compliance mode</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>How you set it</td><td><code>put-backup-vault-lock-configuration</code> without <code>--changeable-for-days</code></td><td>With <code>--changeable-for-days</code> (≥ 3 days)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Can the lock be removed or changed?</td><td>Yes, by principals with <code>backup:DeleteBackupVaultLockConfiguration</code> / <code>PutBackupVaultLockConfiguration</code></td><td>Only during the grace time; afterwards by nobody (not root, not AWS)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Recovery points deletable before retention?</td><td>No, while the lock is in place</td><td>No</td></tr>
<tr><td>Min/max retention enforced on new jobs?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Vault deletable?</td><td>After removing the lock and its recovery points</td><td>Not while it holds unexpired recovery points</td></tr>
<tr><td>Use it for</td><td>Testing, internal guardrails, trusted admins</td><td>Regulatory WORM (SEC 17a-4, FINRA, CFTC), ransomware defence</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p><strong>Distractors:</strong> "enable cross-Region replication" for ransomware (replication copies deletions and encryption, and it stays in the same account); "copy backups to another Region in the same account" as protection from compromised credentials; "MFA on the IAM user" (doesn't stop stolen access keys already in use); "S3 Object Lock" when the data is RDS or EBS (Object Lock is for S3 objects; for AWS Backup recovery points use Vault Lock); "Glacier Vault Lock" for AWS Backup (a different, older S3 Glacier feature); "governance mode" when the stem says "even the root user"; "cold storage" for backups kept 30 days.</p>
<h3>AWS Backup vs service-native backups</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th></th><th>Service-native (RDS automated backups, DLM for EBS, DynamoDB PITR)</th><th>AWS Backup</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Scope</td><td>One service</td><td>Many services, one policy</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cross-account copies, Vault Lock, audit reports</td><td>Limited or none</td><td>Built in</td></tr>
<tr><td>Organization-wide policy</td><td>No</td><td>Backup policies</td></tr>
<tr><td>When to prefer</td><td>Single service, simple needs (e.g. RDS automated backups for PITR)</td><td>Multi-service, compliance, central governance</td></tr>
</tbody></table>` },

    { type: "architect", html: `
<h3>Design checklist for production backups</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Start from RPO and RTO per tier</strong>, not from the tool. Gold: continuous + daily, cross-account copy, restore-tested weekly. Silver: daily, cross-Region. Bronze: weekly. Map tiers to tag values.</li>
  <li><strong>Separate account, separate admins, separate key.</strong> The backup account's vault uses a customer managed key in that account; workload admins have no role there.</li>
  <li><strong>Retention longer than dwell time.</strong> Keep at least one locked copy older than your realistic detection time (weeks, not days).</li>
  <li><strong>Encrypt at the source with customer managed keys</strong> for snapshot-based services, so cross-account copies are possible (M07.06), and share the key with the backup account in the key policy (M07.03).</li>
  <li><strong>Make protection unavoidable:</strong> tag policy + SCP requiring <code>BackupTier</code>; Audit Manager control "resources protected by a backup plan"; Config rule for untagged resources.</li>
  <li><strong>Deny destructive actions with SCPs</strong> in workload OUs: <code>backup:DeleteRecoveryPoint</code>, <code>backup:DeleteBackupVault</code>, <code>backup:PutBackupVaultAccessPolicy</code>, <code>backup:DeleteBackupVaultLockConfiguration</code>, <code>kms:ScheduleKeyDeletion</code>, with a break-glass exception (M06.03).</li>
  <li><strong>Pre-build the recovery account</strong> (network, subnet groups, roles) and rehearse restores into it.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Troubleshooting playbook</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Backup job <code>EXPIRED</code></td><td>Couldn't start within the start window (a previous job on the resource still running, or window too short)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Job <code>FAILED</code>: access denied</td><td>Selection role lacks permissions, or the resource's KMS key policy doesn't allow the role / AWS Backup grants</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copy job fails cross-account</td><td>Cross-account backup not enabled in the management account; destination vault policy missing <code>CopyIntoBackupVault</code>; source encrypted with an AWS managed key; KMS key not shared</td></tr>
<tr><td>Copy into locked vault fails</td><td>Lifecycle outside the vault's min/max retention</td></tr>
<tr><td>Resource never backed up</td><td>Tag typo (<code>backuptier</code> vs <code>BackupTier</code>), resource type opted out in Region settings, or excluded by <code>NotResources</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Plan rejected</td><td><code>DeleteAfterDays</code> &lt; cold transition + 90; continuous backup retention over 35 days</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<h3>Cost traps</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Copies multiply storage:</strong> three copies of everything is three bills, plus cross-Region transfer. Give copies shorter retention than the source where the threat model allows.</li>
  <li><strong>Compliance lock with a huge maximum</strong> commits you to paying for whatever lands in the vault. Keep the max close to the real policy.</li>
  <li><strong>Cold storage for short retention</strong> costs more (90-day minimum) and archived EBS snapshots are full copies.</li>
  <li><strong>Orphaned recovery points</strong> from deleted plans keep their lifecycle; plans deleted without "delete after" lifecycles keep points forever. Audit vaults for points without an expiry.</li>
  <li><strong>Restore tests</strong> create real resources for hours: use small instance classes in overrides and short validation windows.</li>
</ul>` },

    { type: "summary", html: `
<ul>
  <li>Backups protect <strong>availability</strong>; a backup must be point-in-time and out of reach of whatever destroyed the original. Replication is not a backup.</li>
  <li><strong>AWS Backup</strong>: plans (rules: schedule, windows, lifecycle, copy actions, continuous backup) + selections (by tag, ARN, conditions) + vaults (KMS key, access policy, lock).</li>
  <li><strong>Tag-based selection</strong> protects new resources automatically; enforce the tag with tag policies and SCPs.</li>
  <li><strong>Cross-Region copy</strong> = Region DR; <strong>cross-account copy</strong> (same organization, enabled in the management account) = protection from compromised credentials.</li>
  <li><strong>Vault Lock</strong>: governance mode is removable with permissions; compliance mode is immutable after a grace time of at least 3 days, even for root.</li>
  <li><strong>Logically air-gapped vaults</strong> are always locked, AWS-owned-key encrypted by default and shareable through RAM for direct recovery.</li>
  <li>Protect the <strong>KMS keys and the backup account</strong> too, and keep retention longer than attacker dwell time.</li>
  <li><strong>Cold storage</strong> only for supported types, minimum 90 days (DeleteAfterDays ≥ cold + 90); it pays off for long retention, not short.</li>
  <li>Prove it: <strong>restore testing</strong> measures recoverability; <strong>Backup Audit Manager</strong> reports compliance. Org-wide rollout: backup policies (M06.08). Deeper: M20.05 and M33.</li>
</ul>` }
  ],
  drills: [
    { id: "M07.10-d1", q: "A backup rule sets <code>MoveToColdStorageAfterDays</code> to 30. What is the smallest <code>DeleteAfterDays</code> value the API accepts?", answers: ["120", "120 days"], hint: "Cold storage has a minimum.", explain: "DeleteAfterDays ≥ MoveToColdStorageAfterDays + 90 = 30 + 90 = 120." },
    { id: "M07.10-d2", q: "Example 4, option B: 12 monthly points of 400 GB, cold after 30 days, warm $0.05 and cold $0.01 per GB-month. What is the steady-state monthly storage cost in USD? (number only)", answers: ["64", "$64", "64.00", "$64.00"], hint: "1 point warm, 11 cold.", explain: "Warm 400 × 0.05 = $20; cold 11 × 400 × 0.01 = $44; total $64 (vs $240 all warm)." },
    { id: "M07.10-d3", q: "With warm at $0.05/GB-month and cold at $0.01/GB-month (90-day minimum, 30-day months), a point with fewer than how many days of remaining life is cheaper left in warm storage? (number of days)", answers: ["18", "18 days"], hint: "Minimum cold charge per GB, divided by the warm cost per GB-day.", explain: "Cold minimum = 90 × 0.01 ÷ 30 = $0.03/GB. Warm per day = 0.05 ÷ 30. 0.03 ÷ (0.05 ÷ 30) = 18 days." },
    { id: "M07.10-d4", q: "A 400 GB cold recovery point is deleted after 40 days in cold. At $0.01/GB-month (30-day month), what is the early-deletion charge in USD? (two decimals)", answers: ["6.67", "$6.67", "6.67 usd", "6.666", "6.7"], hint: "Bill the days remaining of the 90.", explain: "90 − 40 = 50 days; 400 × 0.01 × 50 ÷ 30 = $6.67." },
    { id: "M07.10-d5", q: "Which Vault Lock mode can be removed later by an IAM principal with the right permission: governance or compliance?", answers: ["governance", "governance mode"], explain: "Governance mode can be changed or removed by principals allowed backup:DeleteBackupVaultLockConfiguration. Compliance mode becomes immutable after its grace time." },
    { id: "M07.10-d6", q: "Which <code>put-backup-vault-lock-configuration</code> parameter turns a lock into compliance mode? (CLI option)", answers: ["--changeable-for-days", "changeable-for-days", "ChangeableForDays", "changeablefordays"], explain: "Setting --changeable-for-days (ChangeableForDays) starts the grace time; omitting it gives governance mode." },
    { id: "M07.10-d7", q: "What is the minimum grace time (cooling-off period) for a compliance-mode Vault Lock, in hours?", answers: ["72", "72 hours", "72h"], hint: "ChangeableForDays minimum × 24.", explain: "ChangeableForDays must be at least 3, so at least 72 hours before the lock becomes immutable." },
    { id: "M07.10-d8", q: "A vault is locked with min 7 and max 400 days retention. A copy action delivers a recovery point with <code>DeleteAfterDays: 5</code>. Does the copy job succeed or fail?", answers: ["fail", "fails", "failed", "it fails"], explain: "Jobs whose lifecycle falls outside the lock's min/max retention fail." },
    { id: "M07.10-d9", q: "Which AWS Backup feature automatically restores recovery points on a schedule, lets you validate them and then deletes the restored resources?", answers: ["restore testing", "aws backup restore testing", "restore testing plan", "restore test"], explain: "Restore testing plans and selections; the restore duration is your measured restore time." },
    { id: "M07.10-d10", q: "Which AWS Backup global setting must be enabled in the management account before cross-account copies work? (setting name)", answers: ["isCrossAccountBackupEnabled", "iscrossaccountbackupenabled", "cross-account backup", "cross account backup"], explain: "aws backup update-global-settings --global-settings isCrossAccountBackupEnabled=true, run in the management account." },
    { id: "M07.10-d11", q: "What is the maximum retention, in days, of AWS Backup continuous backups (point-in-time recovery)?", answers: ["35", "35 days", "thirty-five"], explain: "Continuous backups for supported services keep up to 35 days." },
    { id: "M07.10-d12", q: "An RDS snapshot encrypted with the AWS managed key <code>aws/rds</code> must be copied to the backup account. Which key type must the source use instead? (three words)", answers: ["customer managed key", "customer-managed key", "customer managed", "cmk", "customer managed kms key"], explain: "AWS managed key policies can't be edited to allow another account, so cross-account copies need a customer managed key shared with the destination." }
  ],
  check: [
    { id: "M07.10-k1", type: "single", domain: "D1", task: "1.3", level: 300,
      stem: "A financial services company must keep backups of its RDS databases and EFS file systems for 7 years. Regulators require that no one, including administrators and the AWS account root user, can delete or shorten the retention of these backups. What should a solutions architect do?",
      options: [
        { t: "Store the backups in an AWS Backup vault with Vault Lock in compliance mode and a minimum retention of 7 years", c: true, why: "After the grace time, a compliance-mode lock can't be changed or removed by anyone, and recovery points can't be deleted before retention ends." },
        { t: "Store the backups in an AWS Backup vault with Vault Lock in governance mode", c: false, why: "Principals with the right IAM permission can remove a governance-mode lock." },
        { t: "Enable S3 Object Lock in compliance mode on the backup vault", c: false, why: "Object Lock applies to S3 objects, not to AWS Backup vaults or RDS/EFS recovery points." },
        { t: "Attach a vault access policy that denies backup:DeleteRecoveryPoint", c: false, why: "An account administrator can edit or remove the vault access policy, and the root user isn't constrained." }
      ] },
    { id: "M07.10-k2", type: "multi", domain: "D1", task: "1.3", level: 300,
      stem: "A security review assumes that an attacker could obtain administrator credentials in the production account. The company must still be able to restore its EC2, EBS and RDS data. Which TWO measures meet this requirement?",
      options: [
        { t: "Copy recovery points to a vault in a separate backup account in the same organization, protected by Vault Lock in compliance mode", c: true, why: "Production credentials don't work in the backup account, and the lock blocks deletion by anyone there." },
        { t: "Copy recovery points to a logically air-gapped vault and share it with a recovery account through AWS RAM", c: true, why: "Always locked, encrypted with an AWS owned key by default, and recoverable from another account." },
        { t: "Copy recovery points to a second Region in the production account", c: false, why: "The stolen admin credentials can delete copies in any Region of the same account." },
        { t: "Increase the retention of recovery points in the production account's vault", c: false, why: "Retention doesn't stop an administrator from deleting recovery points." },
        { t: "Enable MFA for all IAM users in the production account", c: false, why: "Good hygiene, but stolen access keys already in use bypass console MFA and the threat is assumed to have succeeded." }
      ] },
    { id: "M07.10-k3", type: "single", domain: "D1", task: "1.3", level: 300,
      stem: "A team wants to try AWS Backup Vault Lock on a production vault. They must be able to remove the lock if the retention values turn out to be wrong, but recovery points must not be deletable while the lock is in place. Which configuration should they use?",
      options: [
        { t: "put-backup-vault-lock-configuration with min and max retention and without --changeable-for-days", c: true, why: "That is governance mode: enforced, but removable by authorised principals." },
        { t: "put-backup-vault-lock-configuration with --changeable-for-days 3", c: false, why: "Compliance mode becomes immutable after 3 days; they'd lose the ability to remove it." },
        { t: "Create a logically air-gapped vault", c: false, why: "Air-gapped vaults are always locked in compliance mode." },
        { t: "Add a legal hold to every recovery point", c: false, why: "A legal hold blocks lifecycle deletion but doesn't enforce min/max retention on the vault; it isn't a vault lock." }
      ] },
    { id: "M07.10-k4", type: "single", domain: "D4", task: "4.1", level: 300,
      stem: "A company backs up Amazon EFS file systems daily and keeps each backup for 60 days. A cost review suggests moving backups to cold storage after 7 days. What should the solutions architect recommend?",
      options: [
        { t: "Keep the daily backups in warm storage, because cold storage has a 90-day minimum and the backups are deleted after 60 days", c: true, why: "The lifecycle can't delete a cold point before cold + 90 days, and paying the 90-day minimum costs more than 53 extra days warm." },
        { t: "Move backups to cold storage after 7 days and delete after 60 days", c: false, why: "The API rejects it: DeleteAfterDays must be at least 7 + 90 = 97." },
        { t: "Move backups to cold storage after 7 days and extend retention to 97 days", c: false, why: "Satisfies the API, but pays for 37 more days of retention than the business needs." },
        { t: "Copy the backups to S3 Glacier Deep Archive with a lifecycle rule", c: false, why: "AWS Backup recovery points aren't managed through S3 lifecycle rules; this adds complexity without meeting the restore model." }
      ] },
    { id: "M07.10-k5", type: "single", domain: "D2", task: "2.2", level: 300,
      stem: "Auditors require evidence every month that critical RDS databases can actually be restored from backup and how long a restore takes. The operations team wants the least manual effort. What should the architect implement?",
      options: [
        { t: "AWS Backup restore testing with a selection for the tagged RDS databases and a Lambda validation function", c: true, why: "Scheduled automated restores, validation, automatic clean-up and recorded restore durations." },
        { t: "AWS Backup Audit Manager with a control that checks resources are protected by a backup plan", c: false, why: "Proves backups exist and comply, not that they restore or how long it takes." },
        { t: "A monthly runbook where an engineer restores a snapshot manually and records the time", c: false, why: "Works, but is manual and error-prone." },
        { t: "Enable RDS Multi-AZ on the critical databases", c: false, why: "High availability, not evidence of backup recoverability." }
      ] },
    { id: "M07.10-k6", type: "single", domain: "D1", task: "1.3", level: 300,
      stem: "An AWS Backup copy action that copies RDS recovery points from a workload account to a central backup account in the same organization fails. Cross-account backup is enabled and the destination vault policy allows backup:CopyIntoBackupVault from the organization. The RDS instance is encrypted with the aws/rds key. What is the cause?",
      options: [
        { t: "Snapshots encrypted with an AWS managed key can't be shared or copied to another account; the source must use a customer managed key shared with the destination", c: true, why: "AWS managed key policies can't be edited, so the destination account can never use aws/rds." },
        { t: "Cross-account copies require the accounts to be in different organizations", c: false, why: "The opposite: they must be in the same organization." },
        { t: "The destination vault must use the aws/backup key", c: false, why: "The destination vault can use a customer managed key; that's recommended." },
        { t: "Cross-account copy is supported only for EBS", c: false, why: "Many services, including RDS, support cross-account copy." }
      ] }
  ],
  cards: ["fc-M07-10-01", "fc-M07-10-02", "fc-M07-10-03", "fc-M07-10-04", "fc-M07-10-05", "fc-M07-10-06", "fc-M07-10-07", "fc-M07-10-08", "fc-M07-10-09", "fc-M07-10-10", "fc-M07-10-11", "fc-M07-10-12"],
  references: [
    "AWS Backup Developer Guide: <em>Backup plans</em>, <em>Assigning resources to a backup plan</em>, <em>Lifecycle and storage tiers</em>, <em>Feature availability by resource</em>",
    "AWS Backup Developer Guide: <em>AWS Backup Vault Lock</em>, <em>Logically air-gapped vaults</em>, <em>Creating backup copies across AWS accounts</em>, <em>Encryption for backups in AWS Backup</em>",
    "AWS Backup Developer Guide: <em>Restore testing</em> and <em>AWS Backup Audit Manager</em>",
    "AWS Prescriptive Guidance: <em>Protecting data from ransomware with AWS Backup</em> and the AWS Security Reference Architecture (backup account)",
    "Cohasset Associates compliance assessment of AWS Backup Vault Lock (SEC 17a-4(f), FINRA 4511(c), CFTC 1.31)",
    "Lessons M04.03 (replication is not a backup), M06.08 (backup policies, central backup account), M07.03 (key policies), M07.06 (encryption per service), M07.09 (S3 Object Lock); forward: M20.05 (AWS Backup in depth), M33 (DR strategies)"
  ]
});

FLASHCARDS.push(
  { id: "fc-M07-10-01", front: "AWS Backup building blocks?", back: "Backup plan (rules: schedule, start/completion window, lifecycle, copy actions, continuous backup) + backup selection (tags/ARNs/conditions + IAM role) → backup vault (KMS key, access policy, Vault Lock) → recovery points." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-02", front: "Why select resources by tag?", back: "New resources tagged e.g. BackupTier=gold are protected automatically. ListOfTags = OR; Conditions = AND (StringEquals/NotEquals/Like/NotLike)." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-03", front: "Cold storage rules in AWS Backup?", back: "Supported resource types only (e.g. EFS, DynamoDB advanced, EBS archive); minimum 90 days; DeleteAfterDays ≥ MoveToColdStorageAfterDays + 90; early deletion is billed." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-04", front: "Vault Lock governance vs compliance?", back: "Governance: no ChangeableForDays; removable by principals with permission. Compliance: ChangeableForDays ≥ 3 (72 h grace), then immutable for everyone incl. root and AWS." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-05", front: "What do Vault Lock min/max retention do?", back: "Jobs (backup or copy) whose lifecycle falls outside the range fail; recovery points can't be deleted before the minimum." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-06", front: "Cross-Region vs cross-account copy: which threat each?", back: "Cross-Region: Regional outage (DR). Cross-account: compromised credentials, insiders, ransomware. Same-account copies don't stop an admin." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-07", front: "Cross-account copy prerequisites?", back: "Same organization; isCrossAccountBackupEnabled in the management account; destination vault policy allows backup:CopyIntoBackupVault; snapshot-based sources encrypted with a customer managed key shared with the destination." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-08", front: "Logically air-gapped vault?", back: "Always compliance-locked vault, AWS owned key by default, shareable via AWS RAM so a recovery account restores directly; optional multi-party approval." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-09", front: "What does Vault Lock NOT protect?", back: "The KMS key (deny kms:ScheduleKeyDeletion), the backup account's own access, and copies that expired before you detected the attacker (dwell time)." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-10", front: "Restore testing?", back: "Scheduled automatic restores (LATEST or RANDOM within window), optional validation via EventBridge + Lambda + PutRestoreValidationResult, auto-delete after the validation window; records restore time." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-11", front: "Backup Audit Manager?", back: "Frameworks of controls (protected by plan, frequency/retention, encrypted, cross-Region/account copy, vault locked, restore time) evaluated via AWS Config; report plans to S3." },
  { id: "fc-M07-10-12", front: "Continuous backup in AWS Backup?", back: "Point-in-time recovery for supported services (e.g. RDS, Aurora, S3) up to 35 days; pair with snapshot rules for long retention." }
);
